KIND offers fast, local Kubernetes clusters for quick, repeatable development and testing without cloud setup overhead. It saves costs by avoiding cloud resources during early phases and provides a fully controlled environment ideal for CI/CD and debugging. The project uses cloud-agnostic Helm charts and GitOps, making it easy to migrate later to managed cloud services like AKS, EKS, or GKE. This approach focuses on showcasing DevOps best practices independent of cloud, with plans to extend to cloud for production scalability and resilience.


1.  default-deny-all - Security Foundation
Why Essential:
🛡️ Zero Trust model - Nothing communicates unless explicitly allowed
📋 Requirement compliance - "All other traffic to database should be denied"
🚫 Without this: Any pod can talk to any other pod = No security

2. allow-dns - Infrastructure Requirement
Why Essential:
🔍 Service discovery - Pods need to resolve mysql-service → IP address
🌐 Kubernetes basics - Without DNS, mysql-service hostname fails
🚫 Without this: Web server can't find database = Complete failure

3. allow-web-to-database - Core Requirement [Web server → Database port 3306 ONLY]
Why Essential:
📋 Direct requirement - "Allow web server to initiate connections to database on port 3306"
🚫 Without this: Web application can't connect to database = No functionality

4. allow-web-server-ingress - User Access Requirement [External traffic → Web server (port 80/443)]
Why Essential:
🌐 Requirement 3b - "The web-page should be accessible from the browser"
👤 User access - External users/load balancers must reach web server
🚫 Without this: No external access to web application = Demo fails

5. allow-web-server-egress [webserver --> Outbound traffic DB port 3306] 
Why Essential:
🔄 Bidirectional communication - TCP connections need response packets
🔗 Database connections - Web server initiates, but needs to receive responses
🚫 Without this: Web server can send to DB but can't receive responses = Connection fails

6. allow-load-testing-access - HPA Requirement [ Load testing → Web server]
Why Essential:
📋 Requirement 6 - "Demonstrate the HPA working with load-testing tool"
📈 CPU load generation - Load tester must hit web server to trigger scaling
🚫 Without this: Can't demonstrate auto-scaling = Requirement 6 fails

7. ✅ allow-web-server-load-testing - HPA Technical Requirement  Web server ← Load testing responses
🔄 HTTP response flow - Load testing sends requests, needs responses
📊 Metrics generation - Responses create CPU/memory load for HPA triggers
🚫 Without this: Load test requests succeed but no responses = No CPU load = No scaling


